SQL fluency

.thinking in data
    .describe what data I want and how it relates; helps see patterns, build insights, and solve problems 
        .don't tell the system how to

    .SQL operates in sets, not steps
        .don't loop through rows - define conditions and let DB engine find matches 
        .don't manually calculate totals - use aggregations and let engine compute them
        .don't manually combine data - define relationships and let joins do the work 

.mental models to adopt for efficient use of SQL
    tables = entities 
    rows = facts 
    columns = attributes/properties of the facts 

.relationships drive insight 
    .most insights come from combining tables 

.filters define the slice you want 
    .the WHERE clause is the scalpel

.aggregation = summaries of reality 

.grouping = patterns 




In short:
    .describe a table in plain language 
    .ask a question that requires filtering
    .ask a question that requires aggregation
    .ask a question that requires grouping 
    .ask a question that requires joining 

    